    "content": "The Space Shuttle Challenger disaster occurred on January 28, 1986, when the orbiter disintegrated 73 seconds after liftoff, killing all seven crew members. A failed O-ring seal in the right solid rocket booster, compromised by cold temperatures, caused the fatal structural failure. The disaster forced a 32-month shutdown of the shuttle program. This video shows the archived news coverage of the Challenger disaster:1mLiveNOW from FOXYouTube• Jan 27, 2026 1m Key Details of the Tragedy Time and Cause: The shuttle broke up 73 seconds into flight at 11:38 a.m. EST, 46,000 feet above the Atlantic Ocean. A cold-induced failure of the O-ring seal allowed hot gas to breach the solid rocket booster, which led to the rupture of the external fuel tank. The Crew: All seven crew members—Dick Scobee, Michael J. Smith, Ronald McNair, Ellison Onizuka, Judy Resnik, Greg Jarvis, and Christa McAuliffe—lost their lives. Last Words: The last known words from the crew came from pilot Michael J. Smith, who said, \"Uh oh,\" followed by a comment from Commander Dick Scobee, which was cut off. Recovery: While the crew cabin was found in the ocean, it is believed the astronauts died from the massive impact (over 200 mph) of the cabin with the ocean, rather than the initial explosion. Impact: The tragedy, watched by millions, caused the US government to suspend the space program for over two years, resulting in massive safety, technical, and management reforms. This video discusses the cause of the Challenger explosion and the investigation:59sABC ScienceYouTube• Jan 27, 2026 59s Aftermath and Legacy Investigation: The Rogers Commission identified the technical cause as the O-ring failure, but also highlighted that NASA management ignored engineer warnings regarding the cold weather. Memorials: Christa McAuliffe, the \"teacher in space,\" inspired continued education efforts, with her lessons eventually being performed in space in 2018. Recent Discoveries: A large segment of the Challenger was discovered on the ocean floor in 2022. Note: There is an upcoming limited series about the Challenger mission starring Kristen Stewart as Sally Ride, which is in development.",
